--TEST--
`Core\Uri::tryParse` answers for every one of the 128 ASCII bytes in a path position — never throwing, and `null` at exactly the bytes `Core\Uri::parse` refuses — spec § 12, ADR 0066 § 3a
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The neighbouring agreement case sweeps twenty-one hand-picked subjects. This
// one sweeps the byte, which is the sweep a grammar is actually written over:
// every ASCII code point is dropped into a path segment and the two members
// are asked the same question about it. What is asserted is counted, not read
// off a line — a `tryParse` that folded only *some* of `parse`'s refusals
// still prints a plausible `admitted=` line and fails the `agreed=` count.
//
// `tryParse` is also asserted **total** here: `parse` throwing 46 times and
// `tryParse` throwing zero is the whole of ADR 0066 § 3a, and the reason the
// class declares no `isValid` (uri.rs's own doc comment: a validator written
// as separate code from the parser is CVE-2024-5458).
string $admitted = "";
int $agreed = 0;
int $read = 0;
int $refused = 0;
int $tryThrew = 0;
int $admittedControl = 0;

uint $i = 0;
while ($i < 128) {
    string $subject = "http://h/a" . Core\Str::fromCodePoint($i) . "b";

    bool $threw = false;
    try {
        Core\Uri::parse($subject);
    } catch (Throwable $e) {
        $threw = true;
    }

    // Caught too, so a byte `tryParse` starts throwing at is a failed count
    // rather than an aborted case.
    bool $nulled = false;
    bool $tThrew = false;
    try {
        ?Core\Uri $u = Core\Uri::tryParse($subject);
        if ($u == null) { $nulled = true; }
    } catch (Throwable $t) {
        $tThrew = true;
    }

    if ($tThrew) { $tryThrew = $tryThrew + 1; }

    if ($threw) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if ($nulled && !$tThrew) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
    } else {
        $read = $read + 1;
        if (!$nulled && !$tThrew) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
        // A control byte is counted rather than printed: an admitted one is a
        // fact worth a number, and putting it in the expectation would put a
        // raw NUL in this file.
        if ($i < 32 || $i == 127) {
            $admittedControl = $admittedControl + 1;
        } else {
            $admitted = $admitted . Core\Str::fromCodePoint($i);
        }
    }

    $i = $i + 1;
}

// The admitted set is RFC 3986's `pchar` plus the two delimiters that end the
// path rather than sit in it — `?` opens a query and `#` a fragment, so a URI
// carrying one still reads. `%` is absent because a `%` beginning no escape is
// refused, and `[` and `]` because they belong to a host and nowhere else.
echo "admitted=", $admitted, "\n";
echo "read=", $read, " refused=", $refused, " agreed=", $agreed, "/128\n";
echo "tryParse threw ", $tryThrew, " times; admitted control bytes ", $admittedControl, "\n";
--EXPECT--
admitted=!#$&'()*+,-./0123456789:;=?@ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ_abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz~
read=82 refused=46 agreed=128/128
tryParse threw 0 times; admitted control bytes 0
